      passage: `Writing a good CV, or curriculum vitae, is essential for finding a job, but many people make simple mistakes that reduce their chances of success. First, always tailor your CV to the specific job you are applying for, highlighting the skills and experience most relevant to that role. Second, keep your CV clear and concise; most employers only spend a few seconds looking at each application, so a long, cluttered CV can be a disadvantage. Third, always check your CV carefully for spelling and grammar mistakes, since small errors can create a bad first impression. Fourth, include a short, strong personal statement at the top, summarizing who you are and what you can offer. Finally, always ask someone else to read your CV before sending it, as a second opinion often catches mistakes you might have missed.`,
