      passage: `Cities like Barcelona, Venice, and Lisbon have experienced a dramatic surge in tourism over the past decade, and while the industry brings substantial economic benefits, a growing number of residents argue that overtourism is fundamentally reshaping their cities in damaging ways. As short-term holiday rentals have proliferated, many landlords have converted long-term residential properties into tourist accommodation, significantly reducing the available housing stock and driving up rents beyond what local residents can reasonably afford. Small, family-run businesses that once served the local community are increasingly being replaced by souvenir shops and tourist-oriented restaurants, altering the character of entire neighbourhoods. Some cities have responded with stricter regulations, including caps on the number of short-term rental licenses and taxes specifically targeting tourists, though enforcement remains inconsistent. Critics argue that without more decisive intervention, some historic city centres risk becoming, in effect, theme parks for visitors, hollowed out of the everyday communities that once gave them their authentic character.`,
