      passage: `As climate change intensifies, an increasing number of people worldwide are being forced to leave their homes due to rising sea levels, prolonged droughts, and increasingly frequent extreme weather events, a phenomenon commonly referred to as climate migration. Unlike refugees fleeing war or political persecution, climate migrants currently occupy an uncertain legal position internationally, as no existing international law formally recognizes environmental displacement as sufficient grounds for refugee status. Pacific island nations, such as Kiribati and Tuvalu, face a particularly urgent existential threat, as rising sea levels could potentially render their entire territories uninhabitable within just a few decades. Some countries have begun exploring innovative, if controversial, long-term solutions, including New Zealand's brief consideration of a special humanitarian visa specifically for climate-displaced Pacific islanders. Meanwhile, international organizations continue urgently advocating for updated legal frameworks that would formally recognize and adequately protect climate migrants, though meaningful political progress on this contentious issue has remained frustratingly slow.`,
