      title: 'Weak Ties: The Power of Acquaintances',
      passage: `Sociologists have long studied the concept of "weak ties", the relatively casual connections we maintain with acquaintances, such as a former colleague or a neighbour we occasionally chat with, as opposed to our close, intimate "strong ties" with family and best friends. Counterintuitively, extensive research suggests that these weak ties often prove more valuable than strong ties in specific situations, particularly when searching for a new job or seeking fresh perspectives and information. This phenomenon occurs largely because our close friends typically move within the same social circles as us, meaning they generally already possess similar information and similar professional connections. Acquaintances, by contrast, usually belong to entirely different social networks, giving them access to a wider variety of information, opportunities, and unique perspectives that our closer relationships simply cannot provide. Psychologists also note that regularly interacting with a diverse range of weak ties, such as chatting briefly with a barista or a fellow commuter, can meaningfully boost daily mood and overall feelings of community belonging.`,
