      passage: `Fatphobia, the fear, dislike, or discrimination directed at people because of their body size, remains deeply embedded in many societies, manifesting in ways ranging from subtle everyday prejudice to overt, systemic discrimination in healthcare, employment, and public spaces. Research consistently demonstrates that fat individuals frequently receive inferior medical care, as healthcare providers sometimes attribute unrelated symptoms to weight alone, potentially delaying accurate diagnoses of serious underlying conditions. In the workplace, numerous studies have found that larger-bodied individuals face measurable disadvantages during hiring processes and are statistically less likely to receive promotions compared to equally qualified thinner colleagues. Body positivity and, more recently, body neutrality movements have emerged partly in response to these entrenched, damaging biases, actively challenging the deeply ingrained societal assumption that thinness inherently equates to health, discipline, or moral worth. Critics of fatphobia argue persuasively that focusing narrowly on body size, rather than genuinely holistic health indicators, ultimately does a significant disservice to public health efforts overall, as it fosters shame rather than encouraging genuinely sustainable, positive behavioural change.`,
